    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen a plugin clone is interrupted for whatever reason, the target directory is still in place with a `.git` folder pointing to HEAD.\nOn the next neovim startup `lock_sync()` scans the plugin dir and creates an empty lock entry for that dir. This results in tagging this dir as `is_bad_lock`.\n`lock_repair()` will be triggered then but `git rev-parse HEAD` can't resolve.\n\n🕵️‍♂️ no validation of `path` here: https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/master/runtime/lua/vim/pack.lua#L990\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nmkdir -p ~/.config/nvim-packrepro\ncat > ~/.config/nvim-packrepro/init.lua <<'EOF'\nvim.pack.add({ \"https://github.com/nvim-lua/plenary.nvim\" }, { confirm = false })\nprint(\"loaded ok\")\nEOF\n\n# 1. normal start: installs and loads fine, prints \"loaded ok\"\nNVIM_APPNAME=nvim-packrepro nvim --headless -c 'qa!'\n\n# 2. leave behind what an aborted install leaves\ngit init -q ~/.local/share/nvim-packrepro/site/pack/core/opt/some-plugin\n\n# 3. every start from now on fails\nNVIM_APPNAME=nvim-packrepro nvim --headless -c 'qa!'\n```\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`vim.pack.add()` should not abort because one directory in the pack directory is unusable.\n\n`lock_sync()` already does exactly this for the neighbouring case — an entry that exists but\nis not a directory goes to `to_remove`, which deletes it.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.12.5\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 26.6\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nwezterm\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nhomebrew",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue! I can reproduce.\n\n> `lock_sync()` already does exactly this for the neighbouring case — an entry that exists but is not a directory goes to `to_remove`, which deletes it.\n\nThe problem here is that checking for correct file system type comes \"free\" performance wise. Checking that every directory is a valid Git directory on every startup is definitely too much.\n\nThe good thing is, running `:checkhealth vim.pack` already shows correct information and steps to repair this:\n\n```\n- ❌ ERROR Failed Git command inside plugin \"some-plugin\". This is unexpected and should not happen. Manually delete directory /home/user/.local/share/nvim-packrepro/site/pack/core/opt/some-plugin and reinstall plugin\n```\n\nI'll take a look at if/how this can be improved automatically in `vim.pack.add`, but I don't see the current status quo of `:checkhealth vim.pack` being the provider of relevant information.",
